# `1.2.3` is one bad number, not `1.2` followed by `.3`.
mode: syntax
---- query
from 'orders.csv' | extend version = 1.2.3, ip = 10.0.0.1
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..58
  PIPELINE@0..57
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    LITERAL@5..17
      STRING@5..17 "'orders.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@17..18 " "
    PIPE@18..19 "|"
    WHITESPACE@19..20 " "
    EXTEND_OP@20..57
      IDENT@20..26 "extend"
      WHITESPACE@26..27 " "
      NAMED_EXPR@27..42
        NAME@27..34
          IDENT@27..34 "version"
        WHITESPACE@34..35 " "
        EQ@35..36 "="
        WHITESPACE@36..37 " "
        LITERAL@37..42
          REAL@37..40 "1.2"
          REAL@40..42 ".3"
      COMMA@42..43 ","
      WHITESPACE@43..44 " "
      NAMED_EXPR@44..57
        NAME@44..46
          IDENT@44..46 "ip"
        WHITESPACE@46..47 " "
        EQ@47..48 "="
        WHITESPACE@48..49 " "
        LITERAL@49..57
          REAL@49..53 "10.0"
          REAL@53..55 ".0"
          REAL@55..57 ".1"
  WHITESPACE@57..58 "\n"

error[DS0003]: invalid number `1.2.3`
  --> query:1:38
  |
1 | from 'orders.csv' | extend version = 1.2.3, ip = 10.0.0.1
  |                                      ^^^^^ a number has at most one decimal point
  |
  = help: if this is a version or an address, write it as a string: `'1.2.3'`

error[DS0003]: invalid number `10.0.0.1`
